# Optimize parameter matching in TableMetaDataContext

## Problem

The `TableMetaDataContext.matchInParameterValuesWithInsertColumns()` method uses a nested loop for case-insensitive parameter matching, resulting in O(n×m) complexity where n = columns and m = parameters.

For each column, when exact matching fails, it iterates through **all** parameter entries to find case-insensitive matches:

```java
for (String column : this.tableColumns) {                    // O(n)
    Object value = inParameters.get(column);
    if (value == null) {
        // ... lowercase lookup
        if (value == null) {
            for (Map.Entry<String, ?> entry : inParameters.entrySet()) {  // O(m)
                if (column.equalsIgnoreCase(entry.getKey())) {
                    value = entry.getValue();
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    values.add(value);
}
```

This becomes expensive with many columns and parameters (e.g., 100 columns × 1000 parameters = 100K iterations).

## Solution

Pre-compute a case-insensitive lookup map, reducing complexity from O(n×m) to O(n+m):

```java
// Build lookup map once - O(m)
Map<String, Object> caseInsensitiveLookup = new HashMap<>(inParameters.size());
for (Map.Entry<String, ?> entry : inParameters.entrySet()) {
    caseInsensitiveLookup.put(entry.getKey().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT), entry.getValue());
}

for (String column : this.tableColumns) {  // O(n)
    Object value = inParameters.get(column);
    if (value == null) {
        value = inParameters.get(column.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT));
        if (value == null) {
            value = caseInsensitiveLookup.get(column.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)); // O(1)
        }
    }
    values.add(value);
}
```

## Performance Impact

- **Before**: O(n×m) - quadratic growth
- **After**: O(n+m) - linear growth  
- **Applications affected**: SimpleJdbcInsert users with many columns/parameters

## Testing

- ✅ All existing tests pass (no behavior change)
- ✅ Verified case-sensitive/insensitive matching logic preserved

This PR addresses critical compatibility issues that emerged after migrating API routes from Pages Router to App Router format for Next.js 15, plus resolves ESLint errors that were preventing successful builds.

## Issues Fixed

### 1. Route Parameter Type Compatibility
Next.js 15 App Router changed route parameters to be Promise-based, breaking the original migration:

```typescript
// Before (caused build errors)
export async function GET(
  request: NextRequest,
  { params }: { params: { table: string; id: string } }
) {
  const { table, id } = params; // Error: params is now a Promise
}

// After (Next.js 15 compatible)
export async function GET(
  request: NextRequest,
  { params }: { params: Promise<{ table: string; id: string }> }
) {
  const { table, id } = await params; // Correctly await the Promise
}
```

### 2. Critical ESLint Errors
Fixed several ESLint errors that were preventing compilation:

**Component Display Name Error:**
```typescript
// Before (ESLint error)
const SelectItem = React.forwardRef(/* ... */);

// After (proper display name)
const SelectItem = React.forwardRef(/* ... */);
SelectItem.displayName = "SelectItem";
```

**Conditional Hooks Violation:**
```typescript
// Before (hooks called conditionally)
export function useAuth(skipAuth = false) {
  if (skipAuth) return { /* early return */ };
  const session = useSession(); // Error: conditional hook call
}

// After (hooks always called)
export function useAuth(skipAuth = false) {
  const session = useSession(); // Always called
  if (skipAuth) return { /* early return */ };
}
```

**Const Assignment Error:**
```typescript
// Before (ESLint prefer-const error)
let [data, setData] = useState([]);
data = data.map(/* transform */); // Direct reassignment

// After (proper state handling)
const [rawData, setRawData] = useState([]);
const data = rawData.map(/* transform */); // Computed value
```

### 3. React Hook Dependency Warnings
Resolved missing dependency warnings in useEffect and useCallback hooks across multiple components to ensure proper re-execution when dependencies change.

### 4. NextAuth Route Export Validation
App Router routes can only export HTTP method handlers. Moved auth configuration to separate file:

```typescript
// Before (invalid route export)
export const authOptions = { /* config */ };
export { handler as GET, handler as POST };

// After (clean route handlers)
import { authOptions } from "@/lib/auth/config";
const handler = NextAuth(authOptions);
export { handler as GET, handler as POST };
```

## Validation
- ✅ Build passes with Next.js 15.5.2
- ✅ Development server starts successfully
- ✅ All critical ESLint errors resolved
- ✅ All API routes maintain backward compatibility
- ✅ Authentication flow preserved
- ✅ Dynamic route segments work correctly

The migration now fully complies with Next.js 15 App Router conventions while maintaining 100% API compatibility and resolving all build-blocking ESLint issues.

